WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.600
Of 100 banded birds, 60 are seen at the colony next year.

2
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.820
Did 40 die? Not necessarily.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.800
Some may be alive and unseen.

4
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.680
Some may be breeding at another colony.

5
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.260
To say whether a population can replace itself, you first need a clean count of who

6
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.280
survives and who reproduces at each age.

7
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.730
So start with a cleaner case.

8
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.590
A cohort is a group of individuals born together.

9
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:40.010
Follow one from birth, and at each age count how many are still alive.

10
00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:45.650
Here is a made-up cohort of females, closed and completely observed.

11
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.730
It starts with 100.

12
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.230
A year later, 80 are alive.

13
00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.810
Then 40, then 10, then none.

14
00:00:54.633 --> 00:01:01.173
Divide each count by the starting 100 and you get survivorship, the fraction of the

15
00:01:01.173 --> 00:01:02.413
cohort still alive.

16
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:06.033
Next, count the deaths in each year.

17
00:01:06.873 --> 00:01:11.653
Divide them by the number alive at the start of that year, not by the original 100.

18
00:01:12.713 --> 00:01:14.773
In the first year, a fifth die.

19
00:01:15.373 --> 00:01:17.633
Then half. Then three quarters.

20
00:01:18.193 --> 00:01:23.393
Then all. In this cohort, the chance of dying rises with age.

21
00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:25.877
Now add the births.

22
00:01:26.697 --> 00:01:30.917
Each surviving female gives birth in a single pulse at each age.

23
00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:34.997
At age 1, 80 females have one daughter each.

24
00:01:35.677 --> 00:01:38.637
At age 2, 40 females have two each.

25
00:01:39.237 --> 00:01:42.197
At age 3, 10 females have one each.

26
00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:45.617
That makes 80 plus 80 plus 10.

27
00:01:47.077 --> 00:01:48.997
170 daughters from 100 founders.

28
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.940
So the net reproductive rate, R₀, is 1.7 daughters per newborn female.

29
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.600
It is counted per generation, not per year.

30
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:06.500
If the rates stayed fixed, above one means the population grows in the long run.

31
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.000
Below one means it declines.

32
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.420
At exactly one, it just replaces itself.

33
00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.120
That is a prediction from assumptions, not an observation.

34
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.150
A table is only as good as its counts.

35
00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:26.050
A bird not seen may be alive and missed, or alive and gone.

36
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.410
Missing is not dead.

37
00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.830
So count the survivors and the births at each age.

38
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:40.970
Survivors and births by age give R₀, and R₀ says whether a cohort replaces itself.
